+For a true martingale \(M\), use finite variation to control the continuous
+martingale part and predictability to control jumps.  On a bounded time
+interval, approximate \(M\) by predictable step processes on finite
+partitions.  Along each finite partition the sampled process is a discrete
+predictable martingale after replacing the filtration by the corresponding
+discrete filtration generated by the past partition times.  The discrete
+predictable-martingale theorem says the sampled values are a.e. equal to the
+initial value.  The locally bounded variation hypothesis makes the sums of
+the missed oscillations along refining partitions tend to zero, while the
+càdlàg property identifies the limit with the fixed terminal value.  Hence
+\(M_t=M_0\) almost surely.  Since \(M_0=0\), \(M_t=0\) almost surely.
